{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Summary of the Wikipedia page on Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was born around 1140 as the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. He became count while still a minor after his father was assassinated in 1152. His early rule was under the regency of his mother Hodierna and his uncle King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. In 1164 he was taken prisoner by the Zengid ruler Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim and held for ransom. His release was secured in 1172 and he resumed his role in Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\nRaymond played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a key figure in the struggle between the royal houses of Jerusalem and the ambitions of the Prince of Antioch. In 1174 after the death of King Amalric of Jerusalem he supported the succession of Baldwin IV despite the childs leprosy. Raymond became a regent for the young king and served as his closest advisor. He worked to maintain stability between the factions of the nobility and the military orders. Raymond also had a strained relationship with Guy of Lusignan who eventually became king in 1186. His opposition to Guys faction led to his temporary exclusion from power.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\nIn 1187 Raymond made a controversial truce with Saladin allowing Muslim forces to pass through his territory to besiege the Crusader fortress at Tiberias. This decision was widely criticized and contributed to the Christian defeat at the subsequent Battle of Hattin. Raymond escaped the battle but soon after died in Tripoli in 1187. His death marked the end of the first house of Tripoli and his successor was Bohemond IV of Antioch. Raymonds legacy is complex; he was respected for his leadership but criticized for his handling of the truce with Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\nRaymond III remains a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states. His reign spanned a period of increasing Muslim consolidation under Saladin and growing internal divisions among the Crusaders. Despite his flaws he was one of the most experienced and capable leaders of his time striving to hold together the fragile alliances that defined the fragile Crusader states. His actions both politically and militarily shaped the region during a critical era.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.1741926521062851}, {"token": "Summary", "logprob": -0.8524835109710693}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0007240534760057926}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.14824289083480835}, {"token": " Wikipedia", "logprob": -6.735097849741578e-05}, {"token": " page", "logprob": -0.38696298003196716}, {"token": " on", "logprob": -1.967538833618164}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -0.0028276958037167788}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.07287787614041e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0004638549580704421}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -9.65590606938349e-06}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -4.768360213347478e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.00025281094713136554}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.023345839232206345}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -4.768258077092469e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4057554602622986}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.20002791285514832}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -6.198863957251888e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0008768765837885439}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0006612736615352333}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.2516897186287679e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.0001546025014249608}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0007308434578590095}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00028880240279249847}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.4742427468299866}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.005964694079011679}, {"token": "1", "logpro